WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:02.002
There's this moment in James Cameron's 

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:04.337
1997 blockbuster, Titanic

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:06.439
where Kate Winslet's character Rose 

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:09.743
notices something about the 
lifeboats that ends up being 

00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:11.059
kind of important...

00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:13.432
"It seems that there are not enough
for everyone aboard."

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:14.814
To which Victor Garber

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:17.684
who played Titanic's architect Thomas Andrews

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.420
responds in a flawless Irish accent:

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.889
"I have built a good ship, 
strong and true."

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.125
"She's all the lifeboat you need."

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:28.461
Since the viewer already knows
how this story ends....

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.931
This line comes off as either

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:34.868
tragic arrogance or some sort 
of bad joke.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:39.139
But it's central to understanding 
how the Titanic was designed...

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:41.975
and how it all went wrong.

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:48.081
The Titanic actually had more lifeboats

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:50.316
than was required by British law.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.452
The Merchant Shipping Act of 1894

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:55.989
required that big ships — those weighing over 10,000 tons

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:59.993
have at least 16 lifeboats that could hold 990 people.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:02.729
Ships only got bigger and bigger 
from there

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:04.764
but the minimum didn't change.

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:07.367
When the Titanic first launched in 1911

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:10.303
the ship weighed 
over 45,000 tons

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:13.206
but still only needed 
16 lifeboats by law.

00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:17.477
Titanic had 20, which, if filled to maximum capacity

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:20.513
could carry a total of 1,178 people.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.082
Nowhere near enough

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:26.119
to accommodate the approximate 
2,240 passengers and crew

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:27.887
on board when the ship sank.

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:30.256
But at least from a design perspective

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:31.591
the builders of the Titanic

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:34.060
had every reason to believe that they had constructed

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.063
the safest passenger ship the world had ever seen.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.898
"She's all the lifeboats you need."

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:40.800
This line might be made up for the movie

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.403
but it's how people talked about the Titanic

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:45.371
even after it went down.

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.073
Let's unpack what that means.

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:49.042
The Titanic was designed to stay afloat

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:51.277
even after taking on serious damage.

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:54.881
The bottom of the ship was divided into 16 compartments

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:57.217
by these partitions called bulkheads.

00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:59.452
If the ship's hull was breached

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.554
these compartments could be sealed off from each other

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:03.022
with the flick of a switch

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:05.358
that closed watertight doors connecting them.

00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:08.194
Once sealed off, the water in the affected compartments

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.197
would rise to the height of the sea called the waterline

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.333
but prevent the rest of the ship from flooding.

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:16.469
The idea was that the giant ocean liner

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.238
even after taking on water

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:20.173
would still be the safest place to wait

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:23.409
as lifeboats methodically ferried 
passengers to a rescue ship.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.312
This bulkhead plus lifeboat strategy 

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:29.315
had worked successfully just 
a couple of years earlier

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:30.950
when a ship accidentally rammed

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.419
straight into the side of the RMS Republic.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.722
Newspaper diagrams from 1909 

00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:38.158
showed how the Republic 
was ripped wide open...

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:40.593
and where it was taking on water.

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:41.995
But the crew remained calm 

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:44.063
and didn't evacuate the ship 
right away.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.499
Their confidence was due in large part

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:49.235
to a brand new piece of technology 
they had on board:

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.271
the Marconi Wireless 
Telegraph system.

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:53.740
When the Republic was hit, its telegraph operator

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.276
tapped out the morse code signal
C-Q-D

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:58.778
the distress call that later became SOS

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:00.513
to all nearby ships.

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.749
A few hours later, a rescue ship arrived

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:04.784
and the crew carefully 
transferred everyone

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:07.787
off the Republic in small groups 
using lifeboats.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.422
The Republic eventually sank

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:12.692
but except for 6 people killed in the initial collision

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:15.094
every single person on board was saved.

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:17.130
The Republic was the first shipwreck

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.265
to make use of a wireless distress signal

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:21.534
and its operator was hailed as a hero.

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.737
This incident seemed to prove that on the busy

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:27.807
North Atlantic route, with other ships always nearby

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:29.976
a combination of careful ship design

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:32.645
and this miraculous piece of new technology

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:35.248
had made disasters at sea a thing of the past.

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:39.018
This 1909 news article, 
The Triumph of Wireless

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:41.354
pretty much sums up the optimism of the time:

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:44.057
"The passenger on a well-equipped transatlantic liner

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.359
is safer than anywhere else in the world."

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:48.494
Just three years later...

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.598
Here's what went wrong.

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:52.799
When the Titanic was built

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:55.168
regulations recommended 
that all passenger ships

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:56.569
ships should be able to remain afloat

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:58.938
with any two adjacent compartments flooded.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:00.473
I talked to Sam Halpern

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:02.976
an engineer and long time Titanic researcher

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:04.310
who created this diagram

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.247
based on data from a 1996 forensic analysis 

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:08.548
of the Titanic's design.

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.950
He showed me how the ship was built to stay afloat

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.953
even with three — and in these scenarios

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:16.122
up to four adjacent compartments flooded.

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:18.591
The key was to keep the ship level

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:21.494
so the waterline never reached the top of the bulkheads

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:23.796
and flooded over into the other compartments.

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.098
These scenarios show

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:28.635
the ship was protected from almost any crash 
imaginable at the time

00:04:28.635 --> 00:04:32.972
 including from rocks, colliding with another ship 
and even hitting an iceberg.

00:04:33.273 --> 00:04:35.575
But the Titanic didn't hit the iceberg head on.

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:38.211
Instead, it scraped along the side of it.

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:41.414
And sonar analysis shows the ship was most likely breached.

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:42.181
Here.

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:44.317
Here, here, here.

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:47.186
And most crucially, here:

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.755
Boiler Room six.

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:51.524
The movie actually does a really good job of explaining this

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:53.826
so I'm going to let Victor Garber take it from here.

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.528
"That's five compartments."

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:56.596
"She can stay afloat

00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:58.831
with the first 4 compartments breach, but not 5."

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:01.734
"Titanic will flounder."

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:03.303
"It is a mathematical certainty."

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:06.339
Flooding the first 5 compartments
overwhelmed the design.

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:08.941
It was just too many for the ship to stay upright.

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:12.278
And as the bow dipped farther into the ocean...

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:14.647
the water flowed over the bulkheads...

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:17.317
flooding the watertight compartments one at a time.

00:05:19.052 --> 00:05:21.621
The Titanic sent out its first 
wireless distress call 

00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:22.989
at 12:15 AM. 

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:25.425
35 minutes after hitting the iceberg.

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:28.528
From there, the messages became increasingly desperate.

00:05:28.895 --> 00:05:30.997
"We have struck an iceberg."

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:33.032
"We are sinking fast."

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.268
and "Cannot last much longer

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:37.370
women and children in boats."

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:41.174
But the nearest ship to the Titanic that night, SS Californian

00:05:41.441 --> 00:05:43.109
never got these messages.

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:45.111
That ship's sole wireless operator

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:47.613
had turned off the radio for the night 
and gone to bed.

00:05:48.081 --> 00:05:49.816
The Titanic's wireless operators

00:05:49.816 --> 00:05:52.485
were communicating with other ships like the Olympic...

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.855
and the Baltic that started to head to its coordinates

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:57.657
but we're a long way off.

00:05:57.657 --> 00:06:01.060
The last message from the Titanic received by our RMS Carpathia

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:05.365
at 1:45 AM was "Engine room full up to boilers."

00:06:06.366 --> 00:06:08.935
Without a rescue ship all the Titanic had left

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:10.103
was its lifeboats.

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:13.306
By the time the Carpathia got there around 4 AM...

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:15.742
the ship had disappeared into the ocean

00:06:15.742 --> 00:06:18.678
taking down more than 1,500 
passengers and crew with it.

00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:21.681
The only survivors were 706 people

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:23.883
who made it in to the Titanic's lifeboats.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.085
The disaster permanently altered 

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:28.521
the public's view on the necessity of lifeboats.

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:30.623
You can see how quickly things changed

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:33.226
when you compare this photo of RMS Olympic

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:36.195
Titanic's near identical twin in 1911

00:06:36.195 --> 00:06:38.831
to this one of the Olympic in 1912

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:41.300
immediately following the Titanic disaster

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:46.639
showing double the number of lifeboats along the top deck.

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:50.410
the biggest impact of the Titanic disaster
had on safety regulations

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:52.211
and ship design was the enacting of

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:56.282
the International Convention for the Safety 
of Life at Sea or SOLAS

00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:59.185
a 1914 international treaty that required

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:02.789
wireless telegraph communication to be active 24/7

00:07:02.789 --> 00:07:06.092
and upped the lifeboat minimum to account for everyone on board.

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:08.494
Today, SOLAS requires cruise ships

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:11.030
to be able to accommodate 125%

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.567
of the ship's capacity in small boats in the event of emergency.

00:07:15.301 --> 00:07:20.339
Ultimately, the Titanic disaster was less 
about a fatal flaw in design

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:22.141
and more about tragic timing 
in the early days

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:24.677
of wireless communication

00:07:24.677 --> 00:07:27.213
and a collision scenario too extreme

00:07:27.213 --> 00:07:28.681
to have been considered possible...

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:31.017
until it happened.

00:07:32.124 --> 00:07:33.775
Oh, and one more thing about

00:07:33.775 --> 00:07:36.395
that foreshadowing lifeboat scene
in Titanic.

00:07:36.395 --> 00:07:39.642
"Waste of deck space as it is, 
on an unsinkable ship!"

00:07:39.642 --> 00:07:42.619
The Titanic was never advertised
as unsinkable.

00:07:42.882 --> 00:07:46.517
Although this 1911 edition of 
the trade magazine, The Shipbuilder 

00:07:46.517 --> 00:07:50.976
did describe both the Titanic and 
the Olympic as "practically unsinkable"

00:07:50.976 --> 00:07:53.048
and there are reports from 1912

00:07:53.048 --> 00:07:55.592
that some of the passengers who 
went down with the ship

00:07:55.592 --> 00:07:57.742
refused to believe it was really sinking.

00:07:58.061 --> 00:07:59.468
But the term only became

00:07:59.468 --> 00:08:01.988
widely associated with the Titanic
in the media

00:08:01.988 --> 00:08:03.237
after the disaster. 

00:08:03.481 --> 00:08:04.393
Thanks for watching!

00:08:04.393 --> 00:08:05.773
I had a lot of fun making this one. 

00:08:05.773 --> 00:08:09.270
Especially with adapting this 
1911 side plan of the Titanic

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.644
to help visualize how the watertight
bulkheads were supposed to work.

00:08:12.644 --> 00:08:15.000
